| The cycle a static pass cannot see: the particle comes from a variable, so
| nothing at the call site names the handler it will reach.
| `handlers::check_cycles` lets this through, and the runtime depth guard
| catches it instead — which is why both halves exist.
|
| What the guard does changed on 2026-08-28 (phase 4): it answers with an
| Exception instead of ending the program. Re-entry is the *emit's* failure,
| so the frame that tried to go deeper gets it back, and the invocations
| already running are untouched — they carry on and decide for themselves.
|
| And what it guards changed on 2026-09-19: depth is bounded, not
| forbidden. A handler may be on the stack more than once — a base module
| asks a linked module, which asks back — and only past sixty-four live
| invocations is the emit refused. So this handler recurses 63 times and
| every one is answered up to the bound; the one past it is the Exception, and it comes
| back up through each frame as a value.
| Was `fail_handler_dynamic_cycle.code`.
Loopy { n } =>
    if n = 0
        return Bottom { }
    again = Loopy { n = n - 1 }
    emit again to this get r
    return Ack { inner = r, n = n }

| Well within the bound: every frame answers.
emit Loopy { n = 10 } to this get shallow
assert shallow ∈ Ack
assert shallow.inner.inner.inner ∈ Ack

| Past it: the deepest emit gets the Exception, and the outer invocations
| ran to completion with it inside.
emit Loopy { n = 100 } to this get deep
assert deep ∈ Ack
assert deep.n = 100
walk = deep
steps = 0
loop
    if walk ∉ Ack, break
    walk = walk.inner
    steps += 1
assert walk ∈ Exception
assert walk.source = "core"
assert walk.message = "handler 'Loopy' is already running 64 times — a handler that keeps re-entering itself is a loop"
| 64 live invocations answered (n = 100 down to 37); the 65th was refused.
assert steps = 64
